## DESCRIPTION
## Nonlinear systems
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject('Calculus - multivariable')
## DBchapter('Differentiation of multivariable functions')
## DBsection('Nonlinear systems')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "parserPopUp.pl");

TEXT(beginproblem());

$mc_yes = PopUp(["?", "Yes", "No"], "Yes");
$mc_no = PopUp(["?", "Yes", "No"], "No");

BEGIN_PGML


Consider the _linear_ map [` L : \mathbb{R}^4 \to \mathbb{R}^2 `] defined by [` L(v) = Av `] for [` v \in \mathbb{R}^4 `] where

>>[` A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\ 5 & 6 & 7 & 12 \end{bmatrix} \quad \text{and} \qquad \mathrm{rref}(A) = \begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 2 \end{bmatrix} `].<<

*Almost no calculations are needed for any part below.* Use [` 2 \times 2 `] submatrices to decide.

1. Does the _linear_ system [` L(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} `] globally define [`(x_1,x_2)`] as a [`C^1`] function [`f`] of [`(x_3,x_4)`]? [_]{$mc_yes}

2. Does the _linear_ system [` L(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} `] globally define [`(x_1,x_3)`] as a [`C^1`] function [`g`] of [`(x_2,x_4)`]? [_]{$mc_yes}

3. Does the _linear_ system [` L(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4) = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} `] globally define [`(x_2,x_4)`] as a [`C^1`] function [`g`] of [`(x_1,x_3)`]? [_]{$mc_no}
END_PGML

$showHint = 1;
BEGIN_HINT
Try to do this by looking at square submatrices of \( A \) or its reduced row echelon form.
END_HINT

$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 0;

ENDDOCUMENT();